  "activity": {
    "id": "act_retrograde_motion_linear",
    "name": "Apparent Retrograde Motion of Mars",
    "description": "A simplified linear AR lab for a 100-level introductory astronomy course. Students first observe Mars's apparent eastward drift, westward retrograde loop, and return to eastward motion against ecliptic constellations. They then compare the geocentric epicycle explanation with the heliocentric explanation in which faster-moving Earth overtakes slower-moving Mars. The sequence links outside solar-system geometry to the night-sky view, includes an embodied overtaking activity, compares Mars with Jupiter and Saturn, and closes with a recap emphasizing parsimony and prediction.",
    "workplace": "wp_retrograde_motion_intro",
    "start": "step_01_observe_mars_loop",
    "actions": [
      {
        "id": "step_01_observe_mars_loop",
        "instruction": {
          "title": "Observe Mars\u2019s Apparent Loop",
          "description": "Learning objective: describe the observed path of Mars during a retrograde episode. Watch Mars drift eastward across Leo, Virgo, Libra, and Scorpio, reverse westward for about two months, then resume eastward motion."
        },
        "enter": {
          "remove_self": false,
          "activates": [],
          "deactivate": []
        },
        "exit": {
          "remove_self": false,
          "activates": [],
          "deactivate": [
            {
              "target": "thing_mars_apparent_path",
              "augmentation": "anim_mars_sky_motion",
              "poi": "retrograde_loop_poi"
            },
            {
              "target": "place_observation_zone",
              "augmentation": "aud_intro_observed_loop",
              "poi": "default"
            }
          ]
        },
        "triggers": [
          {
            "mode": "click",
            "id": "to_geocentric"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "step_02_geocentric_epicycle",
        "instruction": {
          "title": "Test the Geocentric Explanation",
          "description": "Learning objective: contrast the geocentric explanation with the observation. Inspect the Earth-centered deferent and epicycle model and notice how tuned circular motions can reproduce a backward segment in Mars\u2019s apparent path."
        },
        "enter": {
          "remove_self": false,
          "activates": [],
          "deactivate": [
            {
              "target": "thing_sky_starfield",
              "augmentation": "lbl_constellations",
              "poi": "constellation_labels_poi"
            }
          ]
        },
        "exit": {
          "remove_self": false,
          "activates": [],
          "deactivate": [
            {
              "target": "thing_geocentric_epicycle_model",
              "augmentation": "anim_geocentric_epicycle",
              "poi": "default"
            },
            {
              "target": "thing_geocentric_epicycle_model",
              "augmentation": "aud_geocentric_epicycle",
              "poi": "default"
            },
            {
              "target": "thing_geocentric_epicycle_model",
              "augmentation": "lbl_step_title",
              "poi": "epicycle_label_poi"
            }
          ]
        },
        "triggers": [
          {
            "mode": "detect",
            "id": "detect_heliocentric_table"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "step_03_heliocentric_overtake",
        "instruction": {
          "title": "View Earth Overtaking Mars",
          "description": "Learning objective: explain retrograde motion in the heliocentric model. From above the solar system, watch Earth at 1 AU move faster than Mars at 1.52 AU and overtake it about every 26 months."
        },
        "enter": {
          "remove_self": false,
          "activates": [],
          "deactivate": []
        },
        "exit": {
          "remove_self": false,
          "activates": [],
          "deactivate": [
            {
              "target": "thing_heliocentric_orbits",
              "augmentation": "anim_heliocentric_overtake",
              "poi": "earth_mars_orbit_poi"
            },
            {
              "target": "thing_heliocentric_orbits",
              "augmentation": "aud_heliocentric_overtake",
              "poi": "default"
            },
            {
              "target": "place_model_table",
              "augmentation": "lbl_step_title",
              "poi": "overhead_view_poi"
            }
          ]
        },
        "triggers": [
          {
            "mode": "click",
            "id": "to_line_of_sight"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "step_04_link_perspectives",
        "instruction": {
          "title": "Connect Orbit Geometry to the Sky View",
          "description": "Learning objective: predict when retrograde motion occurs from relative positions. Follow the Earth-to-Mars line of sight projected onto the starfield as Earth approaches, overtakes, and passes Mars."
        },
        "enter": {
          "remove_self": false,
          "activates": [],
          "deactivate": []
        },
        "exit": {
          "remove_self": false,
          "activates": [],
          "deactivate": [
            {
              "target": "thing_line_of_sight_rays",
              "augmentation": "anim_line_of_sight_link",
              "poi": "ray_overlay_poi"
            },
            {
              "target": "thing_line_of_sight_rays",
              "augmentation": "aud_line_of_sight",
              "poi": "default"
            },
            {
              "target": "thing_sky_starfield",
              "augmentation": "lbl_constellations",
              "poi": "constellation_labels_poi"
            }
          ]
        },
        "triggers": [
          {
            "mode": "click",
            "id": "to_embodied"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "step_05_embodied_overtaking",
        "instruction": {
          "title": "Generate Retrograde Motion by Moving",
          "description": "Learning objective: explain apparent backward motion as a relative-motion effect. Walk slowly around the floor Earth-orbit circle while keeping Mars in view against the distant stars; near closest approach, Mars appears to shift backward."
        },
        "enter": {
          "remove_self": false,
          "activates": [],
          "deactivate": []
        },
        "exit": {
          "remove_self": false,
          "activates": [],
          "deactivate": [
            {
              "target": "thing_embodied_orbit_walk",
              "augmentation": "img_embodied_orbit_instructions",
              "poi": "default"
            },
            {
              "target": "thing_embodied_orbit_walk",
              "augmentation": "lbl_step_title",
              "poi": "closest_approach_poi"
            },
            {
              "target": "thing_embodied_orbit_walk",
              "augmentation": "aud_embodied_overtaking",
              "poi": "default"
            }
          ]
        },
        "triggers": [
          {
            "mode": "detect",
            "id": "detect_outer_planet_station"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "step_06_compare_outer_planets",
        "instruction": {
          "title": "Compare Mars, Jupiter, and Saturn",
          "description": "Learning objective: compare retrograde loops for different outer planets. Observe that Mars has the largest loop, Jupiter a smaller one, and Saturn the smallest because more distant planets show smaller parallax-like shifts."
        },
        "enter": {
          "remove_self": false,
          "activates": [],
          "deactivate": []
        },
        "exit": {
          "remove_self": false,
          "activates": [],
          "deactivate": [
            {
              "target": "thing_outer_planet_comparison",
              "augmentation": "anim_outer_planet_loops",
              "poi": "loop_comparison_poi"
            },
            {
              "target": "thing_outer_planet_comparison",
              "augmentation": "aud_outer_planet_compare",
              "poi": "default"
            },
            {
              "target": "thing_outer_planet_comparison",
              "augmentation": "lbl_step_title",
              "poi": "loop_comparison_poi"
            }
          ]
        },
        "triggers": [
          {
            "mode": "click",
            "id": "to_recap"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "step_07_recap",
        "instruction": {
          "title": "Recap the Retrograde Motion Explanation",
          "description": "Learning objective: synthesize the lab. Summarize the observed loop, the geocentric epicycle account, the heliocentric overtaking explanation, the line-of-sight connection, and why distant planets make smaller loops."
        },
        "enter": {
          "remove_self": false,
          "activates": [],
          "deactivate": []
        },
        "exit": {
          "remove_self": false,
          "activates": [],
          "deactivate": [
            {
              "target": "place_recap_wall",
              "augmentation": "img_recap_summary",
              "poi": "default"
            },
            {
              "target": "place_recap_wall",
              "augmentation": "aud_recap",
              "poi": "default"
            },
            {
              "target": "place_recap_wall",
